<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Belfast</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Belfast is the capital of Northern Ireland, at the mouth of the River Lagan on Belfast Lough. It grew from a small settlement in the 17th century into a major industrial city of linen, rope and shipbuilding; the Harland and Wolff yard built the Titanic. It became a city in 1888 and the capital of Northern Ireland in 1921, and it was a centre of the Troubles from the late 1960s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belfast.jpg" alt="Belfast" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Davidw1406 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belfast_Skyline_City_Quays.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Belfast stands on the banks of the River Lagan and is connected to the open sea through Belfast Lough and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-channel">North Channel</a>. Its name comes from the Irish Béal Feirste, &quot;mouth of the Farset&quot;, a river whose name means a sandbar or tidal ford. The Farset flowed into the Lagan, until it was culverted in the late 18th century, along what is now High Street, and the early settlement developed at the low-water crossing near the present Queen's Bridge. The site has been occupied since at least the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, and the Giant's Ring, a 5,000-year-old henge, lies near the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1613, under a commission from King James VI, Sir Arthur Chichester undertook the Plantation of Belfast and its surroundings, building a new castle and attracting mainly English and Manx settlers. Scottish Presbyterians followed, and in 1649 an English Parliamentarian army temporarily expelled them in the town's only recorded siege. Their descendants' disaffection with Ireland's Anglican establishment contributed to the rebellion of 1798 and the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> in 1801.</p><p>From the 1820s Belfast expanded rapidly in industry. After a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> boom and bust it became the global leader in linen goods, earning the name Linenopolis, and shipbuilding led to heavier industry. When it was granted city status in 1888 it was the world's largest centre of linen manufacture, and by the 1900s its shipyards were building up to a quarter of all United Kingdom tonnage.</p><p>New industry drew large numbers of Catholics from rural districts to a largely Protestant town, and sectarian tensions grew. They erupted in sustained violence in 1920–22, as Belfast became the capital of the six north-eastern counties that kept the British connection.</p><p>In the spring of 1941 the German Luftwaffe attacked the city four times, damaging or destroying more than half its housing and killing more than a thousand people, the heaviest loss of life in any air raid outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. The Troubles began in the late 1960s, and the British Army was on the streets from August 1969 in the longest continuous deployment in its history.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Belfast is the capital, largest city and main port of Northern Ireland, the second largest city on the island of Ireland after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, with 345,418 people recorded in 2021. A division of working-class Protestant and Catholic districts by barriers remains a legacy of the conflict. The port still has commercial and industrial docks and a reduced Harland and Wolff shipyard, and the Titanic Quarter has been developed on former dockland. Ulster University and Queen's University Belfast are in the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Belfast” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ireland’s experience was different: apart from the linen and shipbuilding industries around <b>Belfast</b>, it remained largely agrarian, dependent on tenant farming and, for the poor, on the potato.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Republicanism, especially Rousseau’s, was central to the French Revolution, and in 1791 the Society of United Irishmen was founded in <b>Belfast</b> and Dublin.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/republicanism">Republicanism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry Halliday Sparling's Irish Minstrelsy (1888) prints the anonymous &quot;Green upon the Cape&quot;, dated to 1798, a longer poem in which a croppy from <b>Belfast</b> travels into exile and is questioned in Paris by &quot;Boney&quot;, Napoleon Bonaparte.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-wearing-of-the-green">The Wearing of the Green</a></li><li><blockquote>Bill Clinton addressed crowds in <b>Belfast</b> and Derry in support of the peace process.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-30">30 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1333 William Donn de Burgh, the Brown Earl, was murdered by his own vassals at a ford near <b>Belfast</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-ulster">Earldom of Ulster</a></li><li><blockquote>The lordship's seat was the castle at Castlereagh near <b>Belfast</b>, and its chiefs ruled through Gaelic law, cattle wealth and Scottish gallowglasses.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-clandeboye">Lordship of Clandeboye</a></li></ul></section>
